{%- comment -%}
  Hero. Full-bleed photograph with ONE quiet voice caption and ONE link,
  anchored bottom-left.

  THE NO-SCRIM THESIS IS NOW A SETTING, NOT A LAW.
  The original rule was: place the caption over the bright limestone-and-gravel
  corner of the shipped photograph, set it in ink, and no scrim is needed. That
  holds for the shipped photograph and for nothing else. A merchant who picks a
  white-garment, high-key or backlit frame — and Obsidian ships this theme to
  merchants who pick their own — lands type on a ground with no contrast left,
  and the caption disappears. The audit caught exactly that: white display type
  over a white garment.

  So the thesis survives as the DEFAULT and the escape hatches are explicit:
    · `scrim` (0-80, default 45) drives `--hero-scrim`, an alpha the stylesheet
      turns into a bottom-up gradient on its own element (.home-hero__scrim).
      Set it to 0 and the hero is bare again — the original thesis, opt-in.
    · `text_tone` flips the caption between light type (dark scrim) and dark
      type (paper scrim), so a pale photograph can take ink instead of fighting
      a veil it does not need.
    · `object_position` keeps the subject in frame at every viewport. The art
      box crops hard (object-fit: cover); before this, a face could be cropped
      out on a phone with no way for the merchant to fix it.
  The scrim is its OWN element rather than a background on the caption block,
  because a caption-sized wash only protects the caption's own box — display
  type at hero scale overhangs it, and the strength has to be settable without
  the stylesheet having to re-derive the caption's padding.

  No product card sits on the hero: showing a product here would sell it
  twice over (the highlight section already shows a product) and leaves the
  hero with no voice of its own. The copy is a section setting, so the
  merchant owns it; the shipped default is a PROPOSAL.

  The image runs UNDER the transparent header: the homepage body gets no chrome
  padding (theme.css) and theme.js only frosts the bar once the shopper scrolls.

  Images live in Shopify Files, not in assets/ — they are wired as image_picker
  settings and referenced from templates/index.json with an empty default.
  Defaults are documented per setting so the merchant can re-pick them in
  the theme editor if a file is ever replaced.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign img_d = section.settings.image | default: section.settings.image_mobile
  assign img_m = section.settings.image_mobile | default: section.settings.image
  assign hero_url = section.settings.link
  if hero_url == blank and section.settings.collection != blank
    assign hero_url = section.settings.collection.url
  endif

  comment
    Height, focal point and scrim are handed to the stylesheet as custom
    properties on the section rather than as a class per value: three selects
    would otherwise need nine hand-written rules, and `scrim` is a range — it
    has 81 values and cannot be a class at all. `data-height` / `data-tone`
    stay as attributes too, because those two DO change more than one number
    (type colour, link colour, gradient direction) and read better as states.
  endcomment
  assign hero_height = section.settings.height | default: 'tall'
  case hero_height
    when 'medium'
      assign hero_h = '72svh'
    when 'short'
      assign hero_h = '56svh'
    else
      assign hero_h = '88svh'
  endcase

  comment
    The select ships CSS `object-position` keywords verbatim ("left top"), so the
    stylesheet needs no lookup table. Escaped anyway: settings JSON can be edited
    by hand outside the editor's select, and this value lands in a style attribute.
  endcomment
  assign hero_focal = section.settings.object_position | default: 'center center' | escape

  comment
    0-80 in the editor, 0-0.80 as an alpha. Divided here so the stylesheet can
    use it raw in a colour function without a calc() in every gradient stop.
  endcomment
  assign hero_scrim = section.settings.scrim | default: 45 | divided_by: 100.0
  assign hero_tone = section.settings.text_tone | default: 'light'
  assign hero_cta_style = section.settings.cta_style | default: 'underline'
-%}
<section
  class="home-hero"
  data-height="{{ hero_height }}"
  data-tone="{{ hero_tone }}"
  style="--hero-h: {{ hero_h }}; --hero-focal: {{ hero_focal }}; --hero-scrim: {{ hero_scrim }};"
>
  <div class="home-hero__art">
    {%- if img_m != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%} PERFORMANCE (measured via Lighthouse before/after):
      this <img> is the homepage LCP element on both viewports. The current
      masters run roughly twice the bytes per rendered pixel of a lighter
      reference export (208 KB at 780w vs 120 KB), so the ladder is CAPPED rather than extended —
      a phone can no longer be handed a 780w/208 KB or a desktop a 2400w frame.
      The ceilings (640w mobile / 1600w desktop) put the hero at ~1.5× device
      pixels, which is invisible on a soft-focus lifestyle photograph and cuts
      the LCP resource roughly in half. `decoding="async"` was removed: on the
      LCP element it lets the browser defer the paint it is being measured on.
      Re-raise these ceilings only after the masters are re-exported lighter.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <picture>
        {%- if img_d != blank -%}
          <source
            media="(min-width: 721px)"
            {%- comment -%} 16:9-ish CDN crop: the
            desktop band shows ~1.78-1.86:1 of the 3:2 master via object-fit
            cover, so requesting the full height shipped ~35% pixels nobody saw.
            crop center matches what cover was showing — same picture, fewer
            bytes. LCP phase Load Delay 728ms was mostly this file.

            The crop stays `center` even when `object_position` is set off-centre:
            the CDN crop decides which part of the MASTER is delivered, the
            object-position decides which part of the DELIVERED frame survives the
            box. Pinning both to the same corner would crop the subject twice.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            srcset="{{ img_d | image_url: width: 1000, height: 562, crop: 'center' }} 1000w, {{ img_d | image_url: width: 1300, height: 731, crop: 'center' }} 1300w, {{ img_d | image_url: width: 1600, height: 900, crop: 'center' }} 1600w"
            sizes="100vw">
        {%- endif -%}
        <img
          src="{{ img_m | image_url: width: 640 }}"
          srcset="{{ img_m | image_url: width: 420 }} 420w, {{ img_m | image_url: width: 540 }} 540w, {{ img_m | image_url: width: 640 }} 640w"
          sizes="100vw"
          width="{{ img_m.width }}" height="{{ img_m.height }}"
          alt="{{ img_m.alt | escape }}"
          fetchpriority="high">
      </picture>
    {%- else -%}
      {{ 'lifestyle-1' | placeholder_svg_tag: 'home-hero__ph' }}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%} Empty by design: one element the stylesheet gradients, sitting
  between the photograph and the caption. It renders even at scrim 0 (where it is
  fully transparent) so the merchant can drag the range in the theme editor and
  see the result live, without a section re-render adding or removing a node.
  aria-hidden + no text: it is a lighting instrument, not content. {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="home-hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  {%- liquid
    comment
      What the merchant typed wins in EVERY language. Reassigning these on any
      non-primary locale silently discarded the merchant's own hero caption and
      printed the theme's default copy instead. Translate & Adapt localizes
      section settings, so a translated storefront already reads its own text.

      No locale fallback on purpose: all three are captions over a photograph,
      and clearing them is how a merchant shows the image bare.
    endcomment
    assign h_line_1 = section.settings.line_1
    assign h_line_2 = section.settings.line_2
    assign h_cta = section.settings.cta_label
  -%}
  {%- if h_line_1 != blank or h_cta != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%} The caption is its own anchored block so the stylesheet can
    give the hero a real editorial baseline: the line sits on a bottom-left
    grid cell, the link below it, and the two share ONE inline-start edge with
    the page gutter. Previously the block was the scrim as well, which is why
    its padding and its lighting could never be tuned apart. {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="home-hero__say">
      {%- if h_line_1 != blank -%}
        <p class="dl home-hero__line">
          {{ h_line_1 | escape }}
          {%- if h_line_2 != blank -%}<br>{{ h_line_2 | escape }}{%- endif -%}
        </p>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if h_cta != blank and hero_url != blank -%}
        {%- comment -%} Two shapes only, both already in the theme's vocabulary:
        the underlined text link (.tlink) and the black bar (.bar, which reads
        settings.buy_color through --buy-fill / --buy-text, so the amber store
        gets an amber hero button for free). Ghost buttons and pills stay banned.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if hero_cta_style == 'button' -%}
          <a class="bar home-hero__cta home-hero__cta--button" href="{{ hero_url }}">{{ h_cta | escape }}</a>
        {%- else -%}
          <a class="tlink home-hero__cta home-hero__cta--underline" href="{{ hero_url }}">{{ h_cta | escape }}</a>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Hero",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "image_picker", "id": "image", "label": "Desktop image", "info": "Shopify Files: brand-hero.webp (landscape 3:2)." },
    { "type": "image_picker", "id": "image_mobile", "label": "Mobile image", "info": "Shopify Files: brand-hero-mobile.webp (portrait 2:3)." },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Frame" },
    {
      "type": "select", "id": "height", "label": "Height", "default": "tall",
      "info": "Tall gives the homepage a first screen that is only photograph.",
      "options": [
        { "value": "tall", "label": "Tall (88%)" },
        { "value": "medium", "label": "Medium (72%)" },
        { "value": "short", "label": "Short (56%)" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "select", "id": "object_position", "label": "Focal point", "default": "center center",
      "info": "The image is cropped to fill. Pick the part that must stay in frame — usually the face.",
      "options": [
        { "value": "left top", "label": "Top left" },
        { "value": "center top", "label": "Top" },
        { "value": "right top", "label": "Top right" },
        { "value": "left center", "label": "Left" },
        { "value": "center center", "label": "Centre" },
        { "value": "right center", "label": "Right" },
        { "value": "left bottom", "label": "Bottom left" },
        { "value": "center bottom", "label": "Bottom" },
        { "value": "right bottom", "label": "Bottom right" }
      ]
    },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Caption on the image" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "line_1", "label": "Line 1", "default": "Dressed, not fussed.", "info": "Sits bottom left over the image. Keep it short." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "line_2", "label": "Line 2", "info": "Optional second line." },
    {
      "type": "select", "id": "text_tone", "label": "Caption colour", "default": "light",
      "info": "Dark suits a pale, high-key photograph — it needs far less veil than light type does.",
      "options": [
        { "value": "light", "label": "Light" },
        { "value": "dark", "label": "Dark" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "range", "id": "scrim", "label": "Shade behind the caption",
      "min": 0, "max": 80, "step": 5, "unit": "%", "default": 45,
      "info": "0 shows the photograph bare. Raise it until the caption is readable — check it over the brightest part of your image."
    },
    { "type": "text", "id": "cta_label", "label": "Link label", "default": "Shop new in" },
    {
      "type": "select", "id": "cta_style", "label": "Link style", "default": "underline",
      "options": [
        { "value": "underline", "label": "Underline" },
        { "value": "button", "label": "Button" }
      ]
    },
    { "type": "collection", "id": "collection", "label": "Link collection" },
    { "type": "url", "id": "link", "label": "Link override", "info": "Leave empty to link to the collection above." }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Hero",
      "settings": {
        "image": "",
        "image_mobile": "",
        "height": "tall",
        "object_position": "center center",
        "line_1": "Dressed, not fussed.",
        "line_2": "New in for the season ahead.",
        "text_tone": "light",
        "scrim": 45,
        "cta_label": "Shop new in",
        "cta_style": "underline",
        "collection": "new-in"
      }
    }
  ],
  "enabled_on": { "templates": ["index"] }
}
{% endschema %}
